---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = "Kitchen & Food Safety Disclaimer | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Read the official Kitchen Safety, Food Poisoning, Fire Hazard, and Appliance Liability Disclaimer for IsItKitchenSafe.";

const disclaimerFaqs = [
  {
    question: "Do the guides on IsItKitchenSafe replace manufacturer user manuals?",
    answer: "No. The information provided on IsItKitchenSafe is strictly for educational and quick-reference purposes. You should always consult your appliance's official manufacturer manual and on-product stamps first."
  },
  {
    question: "Is IsItKitchenSafe liable for damaged appliances or kitchen fires?",
    answer: "No. Users assume full responsibility for inspecting their kitchenware and operating their appliances. IsItKitchenSafe disclaims all liability for any direct, indirect, or consequential damages."
  },
  {
    question: "How should I handle food safety and expiration questions?",
    answer: "For foodborne illness risks and shelf-life safety, always prioritize official regulatory guidance from the USDA FSIS and FDA, and when in doubt, discard food showing signs of spoilage."
  }
];

const disclaimerSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "name": "Kitchen & Food Safety Disclaimer",
  "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/disclaimer/",
  "description": "Comprehensive kitchen appliance, fire hazard, food safety, and manufacturer liability disclaimer.",
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "IsItKitchenSafe",
    "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com"
  }
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} faqs={disclaimerFaqs} excludeArticleSchema={true} availableLocales={['en', 'es', 'pt', 'zh-cn', 'ja']}>
  <script is:inline slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(disclaimerSchema)} />
  <section class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6 py-[96px]">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <span class="text-caption text-signature-coral font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs block mb-2">Legal & Safety Notice</span>
      <h1 class="text-display-lg font-medium text-ink tracking-tight mb-4">Kitchen & Food Safety Disclaimer</h1>
      <p class="text-muted text-[13px]">Last updated: October 23, 2026</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose max-w-none text-body-md text-body leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-6">
      <div class="bg-warning-bg border border-warning-border/30 rounded-lg p-6">
        <h2 class="text-title-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Important Notice to Readers</h2>
        <p class="text-[14px] text-body leading-relaxed">
          The information published on <strong>Is It Kitchen Safe?</strong> (isitkitchensafe.com) is compiled exclusively for general reference, educational, and household informational purposes. Kitchen safety involves complex thermodynamic variables, electrical currents, pressure differentials, and microbiological kinetics. By using this website, you explicitly acknowledge and agree to the terms set forth in this Disclaimer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">1. Precedence of Manufacturer Specifications & Product Stamps</h2>
      <p>
        Cookware, appliances, utensils, and food containers are manufactured with diverse proprietary alloys, plastics, polymers, coatings, and glass formulations. Manufacturing specifications, heat thresholds, and chemical compositions change over time.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium">
        You must ALWAYS prioritize the explicit symbols, stamps, embossed markings, and instructions provided directly by the original manufacturer on the physical product and in the official user manual over any third-party website, including IsItKitchenSafe.
      </p>
      <p>
        If an item lacks a microwave-safe, dishwasher-safe, oven-safe, or freezer-safe stamp, or if the manufacturer's user manual explicitly cautions against a specific use, <strong>do not place the item into the appliance</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">2. Appliance Damage, Fire & Arcing Hazards</h2>
      <p>
        Certain materials can trigger instantaneous, catastrophic hazards when exposed to appliance environments:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
        <li><strong>Microwaves:</strong> Metals, metallic gold/silver leaf trims, aluminum foil edges, and conductive minerals can produce electrical arcing, magnetron failure, and immediate kitchen fires.</li>
        <li><strong>Dishwashers:</strong> High-heat wash cycles and exposed heating elements can warp thin plastics, strip seasoned coatings from cast iron, corrode aluminum, and release caustic detergent residues.</li>
        <li><strong>Ovens & Air Fryers:</strong> Exceeding rated thermal limits can cause polymer pyrolytic degradation, toxic fluoropolymer (PTFE/PFAS) fuming, parchment paper combustion, and glass thermal fracture.</li>
        <li><strong>Freezers:</strong> Water expansion during phase change can rupture unvented glass containers, split rigid plastics, and degrade seal integrity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        IsItKitchenSafe cannot evaluate the individual mechanical condition, wattage, age, wiring integrity, or maintenance of your home appliances. You are solely responsible for operating appliances in accordance with manufacturer safety distances, ventilation guidelines, and voltage standards.
      </p>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">3. Foodborne Illness & Microbiological Spoilage</h2>
      <p>
        Our shelf life, refreezing, and temperature guidance is cross-referenced with public guidelines issued by the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA FSIS) and the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). However:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
        <li>Bacterial pathogen proliferation (including <em>Salmonella</em>, <em>Listeria monocytogenes</em>, <em>Clostridium perfringens</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>) depends on specific domestic variables: ambient room temperature, refrigerator temperature consistency (must remain below 40°F / 4°C), handling hygiene, and initial contamination levels.</li>
        <li>Refreezing previously thawed perishable food is safe ONLY when the food was thawed under continuous refrigeration and has not entered the "Danger Zone" (40°F–140°F / 4°C–60°C) for more than two hours (or one hour if ambient temperatures exceed 90°F / 32°C).</li>
        <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out.</strong> Never rely solely on visual inspection or sniff tests to evaluate perishable food safety.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">4. Thermal Shock & Glassware Fracture</h2>
      <p>
        Glass cookware (including Pyrex, Anchor Hocking, and borosilicate or tempered soda-lime baking dishes) is susceptible to sudden, explosive failure caused by thermal shock—rapid differential expansion or contraction across the material thickness.
      </p>
      <p>
        Never transfer glassware directly from a freezer or refrigerator to a preheated oven or microwave. Never place hot glassware on wet countertops, metal surfaces, or cold sink basins. Always allow hot glassware to cool gradually on a dry silicone trivet or wooden cutting board.
      </p>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">5. No Professional or Regulatory Advice</h2>
      <p>
        The content on isitkitchensafe.com does not constitute professional culinary, engineering, electrical, or medical advice. For critical health concerns relating to suspected food poisoning, chemical ingestion, or appliance-related injuries, contact emergency medical services or your local poison control center immediately.
      </p>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">6. Limitation of Liability</h2>
      <p>
        Under no circumstances shall IsItKitchenSafe, its operators, contributors, researchers, or affiliates be liable for any direct, indirect, incidental, punitive, special, or consequential damages, including but not limited to:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1.5">
        <li>Damage to household appliances, dishwashers, microwaves, ovens, air fryers, or freezers.</li>
        <li>Destruction, melting, chipping, or shattering of cookware, bakeware, glassware, or cutlery.</li>
        <li>Property damage, smoke damage, or fire damage resulting from kitchen operations.</li>
        <li>Illness, hospitalization, or medical expenses associated with food consumption, spoilage, or chemical ingestion.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You assume full, personal responsibility and risk for all actions taken in your kitchen.
      </p>

      <div class="border-t border-hairline my-2"></div>

      <h2 class="text-title-lg text-ink font-medium tracking-tight">7. Corrections & Inquiries</h2>
      <p>
        If you believe an entry contains inaccurate technical guidance or if a manufacturer has updated their product specifications, please contact our editorial review team at <!--email_off--><a href="mailto:editorial@isitkitchensafe.com" class="text-link hover:underline">editorial@isitkitchensafe.com</a><!--/email_off-->. We review and update verified data promptly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
